// Practice quiz: Reading an OpenAPI spec and exploring it in Swagger UI
// Minimum release, November 2026: author-checked, no independent review. Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: spring-boot/spring-boot-basics/reading-an-openapi-spec-and-exploring-it-in-swagger-ui

::o1 operation parts::In an OpenAPI document, where are the status codes an operation can return? {
=Under the operation responses#Correct. They are keyed by status code.
~Under servers#Not quite. That lists base URLs.
~Under info#Not quite. That holds the title and version.
~Under the request body#Not quite. That describes the input.
}

::o1 parameter location::What does in\: query mean on an OpenAPI parameter? {
=The value is read from the query string#Correct. in names where the value appears.
~The value is a database query#Not quite. It is about the HTTP request.
~The value is optional#Not quite. required is a separate field.
~The value is in the JSON body#Not quite. Body input uses requestBody.
}

::o2 springdoc::After adding the springdoc web MVC UI starter, what can you open locally? {
=Swagger UI and the generated OpenAPI document#Correct. springdoc serves both from the running app.
~A production database console#Not quite. That is unrelated.
~A hosted copy on the public internet#Not quite. It is served by your local app.
~A code editor#Not quite. It only documents the API.
}

::o3 comparison::Why press Try it out in Swagger UI when you already have the document? {
=To compare documented and actual responses#Correct. A document can differ from behavior.
~To regenerate the Java code#Not quite. It does not change code.
~To deploy the service#Not quite. It only sends a request.
~To disable validation#Not quite. It does not change server settings.
}

::o3 mismatch action::The document says 201, but the server returns 200. What is the sensible next step? {
=Decide which is intended, then fix the handler or the documentation#Correct. The contract and the code must agree.
~Ignore it, because both are 2xx#Not quite. Clients can rely on the specific status.
~Delete the operation from the document#Not quite. That hides the problem.
~Restart Swagger UI#Not quite. That cannot change server behavior.
}

